/* RmlUi overlay for CNewUIMainFrameWindow's HP/MP/AG/SD/EXP bars + 5 corner buttons -- Phase 1 of
   3 of the third CNewUIObj-tier pilot (2026-09-01). See main_frame.rml's own header comment for
   the full layout-intent derivation and scope boundary (skill list / item hotkeys are NOT here,
   still fully legacy).

   Deliberate simplifications (see NewUIMainFrameWindow.h's header comment for the full list):
   gauge fills reuse the real legacy gauge textures via named sprites (see the "Fills" comment
   below) but stretch that fixed sub-rect to the fill div's dynamically-changing height, since
   RmlUi's decorators don't support a per-frame-bindable UV sub-region the way the legacy UV-crop
   render call did -- not pixel-identical, flag if it looks off in practice. Numeric readouts use
   plain text instead of the legacy digit-sprite atlas. Buttons reproduce only the legacy sprite
   sheet's "normal" and "panel-open" frames (2 of the real 4 -- hover/pressed-hover frame-swaps
   are replaced by a plain brightness filter on :hover).

   2026-09-01: #bars/#buttons/#exp converged into ONE shared transform group after a few rounds
   of feedback -- see MainFrameRmlModel::barsLeft's header comment (NewUIMainFrameWindow.h) for
   the full history. In short: #bars was originally fixed-dp-anchored (this branch's usual
   policy) and visibly drifted out of alignment with the still-legacy center-band chrome behind
   it, which scales with window size via UI::Scaling::BottomHudScale -- a different system
   entirely. Fixed with a bound transform tracking that legacy scale (a CCharInfoBalloon-style
   carve-out). Buttons/exp then turned out to need the exact same treatment, and ended up nested
   INSIDE #bars entirely (see main_frame.rml) so the whole bottom HUD strip -- item hotkeys, bars,
   buttons, exp -- reads as one visually coherent group anchored around the bars, rather than
   three/four independently-anchored pieces. */

/* ---- Bars: #bars's own left/top/scale come from a live-bound transform (see main_frame.rml's
   comment and MainFrameRmlModel::barsLeft's header comment) matching UI::Scaling::
   BottomHudCenterTransform exactly, because these bars sit inside the still-legacy center-band
   chrome, which scales with window size -- NOT this branch's usual fixed-dp-anchor policy.
   transform-origin:left top makes the bound scale() pivot around #bars's own left/top corner, so
   a child positioned at local left:158px ends up on screen at (bars_left + 158*bars_scale),
   exactly UI::Scaling::PositionX()'s formula. Children below use *reference pixels* (px, not dp)
   taken directly from RenderLifeMana()/RenderGuageAG()/RenderGuageSD()'s own constants. #buttons
   and #exp (further down) are nested INSIDE #bars specifically so they inherit this same
   transform for free -- their own positions are plain static reference-pixel values too, no
   separate binding needed. */
/* width/height (2026-09-01): explicit, not auto -- purely a sizing utility, #bars itself paints no
   background/border of its own so this is otherwise invisible. Needed as the gauge tooltips' new
   containing block now that they're #bars's direct children instead of nested inside their narrow
   .gauge-slot (see main_frame.rml's header comment on that move, and .tooltip-hp/sd/ag/mp below).
   640 matches this group's own established reference width (#exp uses the same number for the
   same reason). 39 matches .gauge-slot's own height exactly, so `bottom: 44px` below resolves to
   the identical pixel offset it always has -- only the tooltip's containing block changed, not its
   intended on-screen position. */
#bars {
	position: absolute;
	transform-origin: left top;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 9px;
	width: 640px;
	height: 39px;
}
/* Origin (0,0) -- zero delta from centerTransform's own unshifted position (see main_frame.rml's
   own comment on these two markers). Legacy's item-hotkey/skill-hotkey bands already render
   exactly where they should; this keeps them there. */
#item_hotkey_anchor, #skill_list_anchor { left: 0px; top: 0px; }
.gauge-slot {
	position: absolute;
	/* No overflow:hidden here -- .gauge-fill's height is a plain percentage of this box, which
	   never exceeds 100%, so nothing needs clipping. */
}
#bar_hp   { left: 158px; top: 432px; width: 45px; height: 39px; }
#bar_sd   { left: 204px; top: 431px; width: 16px; height: 39px; }
#bar_ag   { left: 420px; top: 431px; width: 16px; height: 39px; }
#bar_mp   { left: 437px; top: 432px; width: 45px; height: 39px; }

/* Fills bottom-up: height is the *filled* fraction, anchored to the slot's bottom edge, matching
   the legacy RenderBitmap() math (a growing rect whose bottom stays fixed, top moves up). Real
   gauge art via named sprites (the top 39 of each 64px-tall source texture -- the only portion
   the legacy UV math ever samples, see this file's header comment) instead of a flat color;
   `image()` stretches that fixed sub-rect to the fill div's current (percentage) height, so the
   art still stretches vertically as the fraction changes -- a real texture beats a flat block,
   but isn't pixel-identical to the legacy UV-crop technique (which RmlUi's static decorators
   can't reproduce without a many-step named-sprite ladder; flag if this still looks off and
   that's the next thing to try). */
@spritesheet mainframe-gauge-hp-sheet { src: "../../../newui_menu_red.jpg";   mainframe-gauge-hp:   0px 0px 45px 39px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-gauge-hpp-sheet { src: "../../../newui_menu_green.jpg"; mainframe-gauge-hp-poisoned: 0px 0px 45px 39px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-gauge-mp-sheet { src: "../../../newui_menu_blue.jpg";  mainframe-gauge-mp:   0px 0px 45px 39px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-gauge-ag-sheet { src: "../../../newui_menu_ag.jpg";    mainframe-gauge-ag:   0px 0px 16px 39px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-gauge-sd-sheet { src: "../../../newui_menu_sd.jpg";    mainframe-gauge-sd:   0px 0px 16px 39px; }

.gauge-fill {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 0;
	width: 100%;
}
/* Legacy-only element (see main_frame.rml's own comment on the two-fill-divs-per-gauge pattern) --
   legacy keeps the original bottom-up .gauge-fill-vertical div, hides modern's new left-to-right
   .gauge-fill-horizontal one entirely. */
.gauge-fill-horizontal { display: none; }
.gauge-fill-hp { decorator: image(mainframe-gauge-hp); }
.gauge-fill-hp.poisoned { decorator: image(mainframe-gauge-hp-poisoned); } /* eDeBuff_Poison swap */
.gauge-fill-mp { decorator: image(mainframe-gauge-mp); }
.gauge-fill-ag { decorator: image(mainframe-gauge-ag); }
.gauge-fill-sd { decorator: image(mainframe-gauge-sd); }

/* Positioned INSIDE the bar, near its bottom -- matches the legacy RenderNumber() calls (all 4
   land at reference y=462, which is 9px above each bar's own bottom edge at y=471/470). A
   negative bottom here (drawing below the bar) was the earlier bug that put the numbers on top
   of the exp bar underneath -- see this file's own history if this needs revisiting.

   2026-09-01: plain white text at 9px was hard to read against the gauge art underneath it
   (legibility feedback) -- bold + a native RmlUi font-effect outline (built into the engine, see
   ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/FontEffectOutline.cpp/Factory.cpp's default "outline" instancer;
   no prior window in this theme has used font-effect yet, this is the first) stand in for the
   legacy digit-sprite atlas's own baked-in dark edge. */
.gauge-num {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
	width: 100%;
	text-align: center;
	color: #ffffff;
	font-weight: bold;
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
	white-space: nowrap;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* Same hover-tooltip convention as mu_helper_bar.rcss's .tooltip -- font declared directly here
   rather than relying on inheritance (see layout-and-scaling.md's "Known RmlUi quirk"). Every
   .tooltip in this file lives inside one of the three bound-transform:scale() containers (#bars/
   #buttons/#exp -- see #bars's own comment), so every length here is px, not dp: a dp length
   would be scaled TWICE (once by UIScalePercent, again by the ancestor's bound scale()).
   `transform: translateX(-50%)` centers the tooltip around whatever `left` is set to; `bottom` is
   set per-container below (bars/buttons sit near the bottom of the screen so their tooltips open
   upward; exp's own override sits lower still, right above the bar).
   2026-09-01: NO `left: 50%` here anymore (was, until this rule). Confirmed RmlUi engine
   limitation, not a CSS mistake: LayoutDetails::GetShrinkToFitWidth (ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/
   Layout/LayoutDetails.cpp) hard-clamps an absolutely positioned element's shrink-to-fit width to
   its CONTAINING BLOCK's width (`Math::Min(shrink_to_fit_width, available_width)`) -- with
   .tooltip nested inside its 16-45px-wide trigger, THAT trigger was the containing block, so the
   background could never grow past it regardless of text length (feedback: "the tooltip fill
   seems to take the size relative to the button ... we need to make it fill the whole text").
   Fixed by moving every .tooltip out to be a SIBLING of its trigger instead of a descendant (see
   main_frame.rml's header comment) -- its containing block is now #bars/#buttons, both given an
   explicit generous width for exactly this reason, so shrink-to-fit has real room to work with.
   Each tooltip now sets its own explicit `left` (see .tooltip-hp/sd/ag/mp/cshop/etc. below) instead
   of a shared 50% -- that always centered against the narrow trigger's own width; against the new,
   much wider containing block it would land in the wrong place. */
.tooltip {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
	white-space: nowrap;
	background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 200);
	color: #ffffff;
	padding: 2px 4px;
	border-radius: 3px;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 11px;
	pointer-events: none;
}
/* Explicit per-tooltip horizontal center (bar's own left + half its width), replacing the old
   shared `left: 50%` (see .tooltip's own comment just above for why). */
.tooltip-hp { left: 180.5px; }  /* bar_hp:   left 158 + width 45 / 2 */
.tooltip-sd { left: 212px;   }  /* bar_sd:   left 204 + width 16 / 2 */
.tooltip-ag { left: 428px;   }  /* bar_ag:   left 420 + width 16 / 2 */
.tooltip-mp { left: 459.5px; }  /* bar_mp:   left 437 + width 45 / 2 */
/* z-index (first use in this codebase, native RmlUi property -- StyleSheetSpecification.cpp
   registers PropertyId::ZIndex with "auto"/number parsers). See themes/modern/main_frame.rcss's
   identical comment for the full reasoning: every popped-up tooltip is one of several same-parent
   siblings that ALSO always paints its own background (real sprite art in this theme), so a
   tooltip wide enough to overflow its own local x-range gets partially painted over by a later
   sibling's opaque art (painted after it in DOM order). Applied directly to each tooltip now (it's
   a sibling of its trigger, not a descendant -- see main_frame.rml's header comment), not the
   trigger itself -- raising the trigger no longer also raises the tooltip now that they're
   siblings rather than parent/child.
   2026-09-01: selectors rewritten from `.gauge-slot:hover .tooltip` (descendant) to
   `#bar_hp:hover ~ .tooltip-hp` etc. (general sibling, RCSS `~` -- StyleSheetParser.cpp/
   StyleSheetNode.cpp confirm it's actually implemented, not just parsed) -- required by the same
   nested-to-sibling move (main_frame.rml), independent of the z-index change above. */
#bar_hp:hover ~ .tooltip-hp,
#bar_sd:hover ~ .tooltip-sd,
#bar_ag:hover ~ .tooltip-ag,
#bar_mp:hover ~ .tooltip-mp,
#exp:hover .tooltip {
	display: block;
	z-index: 1;
}
#bars .tooltip { bottom: 44px; }

/* ---- Buttons: nested inside #bars (main_frame.rml) so they inherit that one bound transform
   for free -- no separate binding needed. #buttons itself uses a plain static reference-pixel
   position (488,429 -- the reference canvas's right band, 152x41, starts right where the mana
   bar's own right edge ends at x=482), anchoring the whole group next to the mana bar instead of
   the legacy window-right-edge anchor. Each button's own left offset is its legacy x minus this
   band's left edge (488). */
@spritesheet mainframe-btn-cshop-sheet   { src: "../../../partCharge1/newui_menu_Bt05.jpg"; mainframe-btn-cshop-normal: 0px 0px 30px 41px; mainframe-btn-cshop-open: 0px 82px 30px 41px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-btn-chainfo-sheet { src: "../../../partCharge1/newui_menu_Bt01.jpg"; mainframe-btn-chainfo-normal: 0px 0px 30px 41px; mainframe-btn-chainfo-open: 0px 82px 30px 41px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-btn-myinven-sheet { src: "../../../partCharge1/newui_menu_Bt02.jpg"; mainframe-btn-myinven-normal: 0px 0px 30px 41px; mainframe-btn-myinven-open: 0px 82px 30px 41px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-btn-friend-sheet  { src: "../../../partCharge1/newui_menu_Bt03.jpg"; mainframe-btn-friend-normal: 0px 0px 30px 41px; mainframe-btn-friend-open: 0px 82px 30px 41px; }
@spritesheet mainframe-btn-window-sheet  { src: "../../../partCharge1/newui_menu_Bt04.jpg"; mainframe-btn-window-normal: 0px 0px 30px 41px; mainframe-btn-window-open: 0px 82px 30px 41px; }

/* width/height (2026-09-01): same reasoning as #bars's own identical addition above -- explicit,
   invisible (no background/border here), needed as the button tooltips' new containing block now
   that they're #buttons's direct children instead of nested inside their narrow .frame-btn (see
   main_frame.rml's header comment). 300 is comfortably wider than any realistic button tooltip
   string; 41 matches .frame-btn's own height exactly, so `bottom: 44px` (#bars .tooltip, above)
   still resolves to the identical pixel offset it always has. */
#buttons {
	position: absolute;
	left: 488px;
	top: 429px;
	width: 300px;
	height: 41px;
}
.frame-btn {
	position: absolute;
	top: 0px;
	width: 30px;
	height: 41px;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.frame-btn:hover { filter: brightness(1.25); }

/* Legacy-only element (see themes/modern/main_frame.rcss's own comment) -- modern's flat buttons
   need a plain-letter monogram since they have no icon art of their own (same convention as
   login_main.rcss's M/C buttons); legacy's real sprite art already has the icon baked in. */
.btn-icon-label { display: none; }

/* Modern-only decorative outlines around the still-legacy item-hotkey/skill icons (see
   main_frame.rml's own comment on #item_slots/#skill_slots) -- legacy's real sprite chrome
   (RenderLeftFrame()/RenderCenterFrame() in NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp) already frames these
   slots, so nothing extra is drawn here. .slot-frame/.slot-fill (the shared modern-theme classes
   main_frame.rml also puts on .gauge-slot/.frame-btn) are likewise unused in this theme --
   legacy styles those elements directly by id/class below. */
#item_slots, #skill_slots { display: none; }

/* 2026-09-02 redesign #7 (see main_frame.rml's own comment on #buttons_top) -- legacy keeps its
   original bottom-HUD button row (#buttons, unchanged) and hides this second top-right copy
   entirely. */
#buttons_top { display: none; }

/* 2026-09-02 redesign #8 (see main_frame.rml's own comment on #gauge_bg_left/right) -- legacy's
   own bars already sit against real sprite/texture chrome, no gap to fill. */
#gauge_bg_left, #gauge_bg_right { display: none; }

/* Legacy-only element (see main_frame.rml's own comment) -- legacy's real IMAGE_MENU_1 sprite art
   already has "Q/W/E/R" baked in above each potion slot. */
.slot-hotkey-label { display: none; }

#btn_cshop    { left: 1px;   decorator: image(mainframe-btn-cshop-normal); }
#btn_cshop.open    { decorator: image(mainframe-btn-cshop-open); }
#btn_chainfo  { left: 31px;  decorator: image(mainframe-btn-chainfo-normal); }
#btn_chainfo.open  { decorator: image(mainframe-btn-chainfo-open); }
#btn_myinven  { left: 61px;  decorator: image(mainframe-btn-myinven-normal); }
#btn_myinven.open  { decorator: image(mainframe-btn-myinven-open); }
#btn_friend   { left: 91px;  decorator: image(mainframe-btn-friend-normal); }
#btn_friend.open   { decorator: image(mainframe-btn-friend-open); }
#btn_window   { left: 121px; decorator: image(mainframe-btn-window-normal); }
#btn_window.open   { decorator: image(mainframe-btn-window-open); }

/* Explicit per-tooltip horizontal center (button's own left + half its 30px width), replacing the
   old shared `left: 50%` -- see .tooltip's own comment (near the top of this file) for why a
   shared 50% stopped being correct once these tooltips moved to be #buttons's direct children
   instead of nested inside their .frame-btn. `bottom: 44px` (#bars .tooltip, above) already covers
   these too via the plain descendant selector -- #buttons's own height (41px, set above) matches
   .frame-btn's own height, so it resolves to the same pixel offset as before. */
.tooltip-cshop    { left: 16px;  }  /* btn_cshop:   left 1  + width 30 / 2 */
.tooltip-chainfo  { left: 46px;  }  /* btn_chainfo: left 31 + width 30 / 2 */
.tooltip-myinven  { left: 76px;  }  /* btn_myinven: left 61 + width 30 / 2 */
.tooltip-friend   { left: 106px; }  /* btn_friend:  left 91 + width 30 / 2 */
.tooltip-window   { left: 136px; }  /* btn_window:  left 121 + width 30 / 2 */
/* Sibling combinator, not descendant -- see #bar_hp:hover's identical rule (near the top of this
   file) for the full reasoning (both the shrink-to-fit-width fix and why z-index moved here). */
#btn_cshop:hover ~ .tooltip-cshop,
#btn_chainfo:hover ~ .tooltip-chainfo,
#btn_myinven:hover ~ .tooltip-myinven,
#btn_friend:hover ~ .tooltip-friend,
#btn_window:hover ~ .tooltip-window {
	display: block;
	z-index: 1;
}

/* Char-info quest-alert / friend mail-chat-alert blink dot -- replaces the legacy frame-swap
   blink (RenderCharInfoButton()/RenderFriendButtonState()) with the same bound-boolean-plus-dot
   convention used elsewhere (e.g. mu_helper_bar.rcss). px, not dp, same reason as the tooltip
   above; border-radius stays a fixed px value on an 8px box (no confirmed percentage support). */
.alert-dot {
	position: absolute;
	top: -2px;
	right: -2px;
	width: 8px;
	height: 8px;
	border-radius: 4px;
	background-color: #ffd700;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* ---- EXP: nested inside #bars, same as #buttons -- plain static reference-pixel position, no
   own binding. Spans reference x=0 (the item-hotkey band's own start) to x=640 (the button
   group's own end, 488+152) per explicit feedback, not the legacy EXP band's real full-window-
   width span -- #exp's local width is exactly this shared group's own 640-reference-unit width.
   top:470px is the reference canvas's own EXP band position (kExperienceTop). Track is inset
   2px/9px matching the legacy fill rect's x=2, width=629 within that 640px band (640-2-629=9). */
#exp {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	top: 470px;
	width: 640px;
	height: 10px;
}
/* Track background -- without this, an empty/low fraction left the scene visible straight through
   the bar (feedback: "no background, ... you can see through the scene"). Legacy's own gauge
   frame art (drawn by IMAGE_MENU_1/2/3, still rendered by RenderCenterFrame() -- out of this
   pilot's scope, see this file's own header comment) normally sits behind this at reference
   scale, but doesn't reach every window size/theme reliably, so a real background here removes
   the dependency instead of just relying on whatever happens to be underneath.
   2026-09-01: `right` was 9px, copied from the ORIGINAL legacy RenderExperience()'s own fill rect
   (x=2, width=629 within the real, full-window-spanning 640px band that inset was tuned for --
   see this file's #exp comment just above). That reserved end margin doesn't apply to #exp's OWN
   width here, which is deliberately redefined to end exactly at the button group's own right edge
   (488+121+30=639, ~="640) -- left at 9px, the track fell visibly short of that edge (feedback:
   "the exp bar's length is off compared to the edge of the menu button"). 1px instead --
   effectively flush; fine-tune further only if it still reads as off at actual scale. */
#exp_track {
	position: absolute;
	left: 2px;
	right: 1px;
	top: 0;
	bottom: 0;
	background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
	border: 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 200);
}
/* Real gauge art, not a flat color (legacy only -- modern stays flat/programmatic, its own
   established convention, see themes/modern/main_frame.rcss). Legacy RenderExperience()'s own UV
   math (0,0)-(6/8,4/4) samples the FULL texture height and stretches horizontally to whatever the
   current fill width is every frame -- unlike the vertical gauge bars above, RmlUi's image()
   stretch-to-fit is actually what the legacy code already does here, no extra distortion
   introduced. Rect dimensions (6x4) are the REAL decoded source pixels (src/bin/Data/Interface/
   newui_Exbar.OZJ, confirmed by stripping its 24-byte OZJ header and reading the JPEG's own SOF
   marker) -- NOT a guess like the first attempt at this rect (192x64, assuming a much larger
   padded texture), which read wildly out-of-bounds pixels from the real 8x4-padded texture and
   showed as visual noise. */
@spritesheet mainframe-gauge-exp-sheet { src: "../../../newui_exbar.jpg"; mainframe-gauge-exp: 0px 0px 6px 4px; }
#exp_fill {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
	height: 100%;
	decorator: image(mainframe-gauge-exp);
}
/* px, not dp -- #exp sits inside a bound transform:scale() now, see #bars's identical note.
   2026-09-01: was `top: -10px` (above #exp entirely, overlapping the bars/buttons row) -- moved
   inside the 10px-tall band itself, vertically centered against the fill, since the whole point
   is to show which of the 10 deciles (0-9) the fill bar is currently on (feedback: "does not have
   the counter for which tenth it already is"). Same outline treatment as .gauge-num for the same
   legibility reason. */
/* 2026-09-01: `right` was 3px, i.e. INSIDE #exp's own reserved right margin but OUTSIDE
   #exp_track -- the digit was landing to the right of the actual bar, not inside it (feedback:
   "currently it is displayed after the bar", wanted "inside, similar to mana and hp"). Kept in
   sync with #exp_track's own `right` inset just above (now 1px, was 9px -- see that rule's
   comment): track's inset + 3px padding puts the digit's right edge 3px inside the track's right
   edge instead of past it. Same fix as themes/modern/main_frame.rcss's identical #exp_digit rule. */
#exp_digit {
	position: absolute;
	right: 4px;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translateY(-50%);
	color: #ffffff;
	font-weight: bold;
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 9px;
	pointer-events: none;
}
#exp_tooltip {
	bottom: 12px;
	left: 50%;
}
/* Show rule for this one lives near the top of the file now (folded into the .tooltip-hp/etc.
   sibling-combinator block, since it also needed the z-index addition) -- #exp_tooltip itself was
   never affected by the shrink-to-fit-width bug (nested inside #exp, which is already 640px wide,
   not one of the narrow triggers), so its DOM position/selector style didn't need to change. */
